p(31,3,"For a few days all went on well. I found that my groom",
 "At first the groom does his job well, but then the horse's oats become fewer and fewer. The horse becomes weaker, but cannot complain.",
 "For a few days all went well. I found that my groom knew his job. He kept the stable clean and full of fresh air, and he cleaned me well, and was always gentle. He had been a hostler in one of the great hotels in Bath. He had given that up, and now he grew fruit and vegetables to sell at the market, and his wife raised and fed up birds and rabbits for sale. After a while it seemed to me that my oats were coming very short. I had the beans, but bran was mixed with them instead of oats, of which there were very few, certainly not more than a quarter of what there should have been. In two or three weeks this began to show in my strength and spirits. The grass food, although very good, was not enough to keep me in good shape without corn. But I could not complain or make my needs known. So it went on for about two months, and I wondered that my master did not see that something was wrong. But one afternoon he rode out into the country to see a friend of his, a gentleman farmer who lived on the road to Wells.",
 ["thoroughly","hostler","cultivated","bred","fattened","poultry","tell upon","condition"])
